      "body": "## Problem\n\nOn a machine with libtpu installed but without `tpu_inference`,\n`vllm/platforms/tpu.py` only logs an error and continues, leaving\n`TpuPlatform` and `USE_TPU_INFERENCE` undefined. Platform resolution\n(`vllm/platforms/__init__.py` -> `tpu_platform_plugin()` returns\n`\"vllm.platforms.tpu.TpuPlatform\"` -> lazy `resolve_obj_by_qualname`)\nthen fails later with a confusing `AttributeError`, and the user never\nsees the \"please install tpu_inference\" guidance from the log.\n\n## Change\n\n- `vllm/platforms/tpu.py`: the `except ImportError` branch now raises an\n  `ImportError` with installation guidance, chained from the original\n  import error (`raise ... from e`), so the failure surfaces immediately\n  at import time.\n- No platform detection logic is changed. `USE_TPU_INFERENCE` remains\n  referenced only by this module (verified via code search).\n- `tests/platforms/test_tpu.py`: regression test simulating a missing\n  `tpu_inference` import and asserting the helpful `ImportError`.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Reproduced the old behavior with an isolated import simulation: the old\n  module imports silently with `tpu_inference` absent, and accessing\n  `TpuPlatform` raises `AttributeError`.\n- With the new module: missing package ->\n  `ImportError: tpu_inference not found, please install tpu_inference to\n  run vLLM on TPU` (chained from `ModuleNotFoundError`); package present\n  -> imports fine with `USE_TPU_INFERENCE = True`.\n- Note: I could not run the new pytest file against the full vLLM test\n  suite or on TPU hardware in this environment; the test is provided for\n  CI.\n",
